---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// DRAFT — written from what's already public on this site, the Four Laws
// about page, and the essay in /writing. Jon has not reviewed or corrected it.
//
// What's asserted here is only what the sites already say publicly. Everything
// that would normally carry an About page — years in the field, named employers,
// client names, engagement sizes, credentials — is deliberately absent rather
// than invented. Those are the parts worth adding, and they're the parts only
// Jon can supply. See the TODO block below.
//
// TODO before this goes live:
//   - How long you've been doing this, and in what capacity.
//   - Where you do it from (Process Pro Consulting, if you want that named here
//     — it currently appears nowhere on either site, which is a deliberate
//     choice worth making consciously rather than by omission).
//   - One or two concrete engagements, describable without naming the client:
//     "a 40-person agency whose quote-to-cash ran nine days" beats any adjective.
//   - Whether the pastoral/seminary track belongs on this page. It's a real part
//     of how you think about institutions and people inside them, and it's
//     either a differentiator or a distraction depending on who you're writing
//     for. Your call, not mine.

const title = 'About — Jon Martin';
const description =
	'Jon Martin writes diagnoses of why systems and org structures keep breaking in founder-led professional services firms — and why the last three fixes did not hold.';

const schema = {
	'@context': 'https://schema.org',
	'@type': 'AboutPage',
	'@id': 'https://thejonmartin.com/about/',
	url: 'https://thejonmartin.com/about/',
	name: title,
	description,
	mainEntity: { '@id': 'https://thejonmartin.com/#jon' },
};
---

<Base title={title} description={description} schema={schema}>
	<main id="main">
		<section class="head">
			<p class="label kicker">About</p>
			<h1>I diagnose. I don't implement.</h1>
			<p class="subhead">
				Most people who get called into a broken business are there to build
				something. That's the problem — the last three things you built are why
				you're calling.
			</p>
		</section>

		<hr class="hairline" />

		<section>
			<h2>What I actually do</h2>
			<p>
				I work in revenue operations — systems integration and subscription
				management, mostly for SaaS and professional services firms. The job
				looks like tooling from the outside. It isn't. It's variety management:
				deciding what complexity a business should absorb, what it should
				refuse, and whether the answer is new tooling or a redrawn boundary.
			</p>
			<p>
				That distinction is the whole reason this site exists. A firm that
				can't quote consistently doesn't have a CPQ problem. A firm whose
				reports nobody trusts doesn't have a dashboard problem. Those are
				symptoms of a structure that produces them reliably, and replacing the
				tool leaves the structure intact — which is why the new tool breaks in
				the same place the old one did.
			</p>
		</section>

		<hr class="hairline" />

		<section>
			<h2>Why a second opinion, and not a project</h2>
			<p>
				The highest-leverage move in operations is usually to move a team
				boundary, and that's almost never available to the person brought in to
				fix the process. I do a lot of my work as an external designer — hired
				to fix the system, not to redraw the org.
			</p>
			<p>
				So the honest deliverable is a written diagnosis: here's what's actually
				breaking, here's the constraint you can't remove, here's what it costs
				you a year, and here's the best system available inside it. Naming a
				constraint you can't remove isn't failure. Pretending you can automate
				around it is.
			</p>
		</section>

		<hr class="hairline" />

		<section>
			<h2>Where the framework comes from</h2>
			<p>
				Four systems laws do most of the work: Ashby's Law of Requisite Variety,
				Conway's Law, Brooks's Law, and the Reverse Conway Maneuver — with
				Beer's Viable System Model behind them. I've written the whole thing up
				as a standing reference rather than re-explaining it per engagement.
			</p>
			<p>
				<a href="https://fourlaws.thejonmartin.com">
					Four Laws of Complex System Design &rarr;
				</a>
			</p>
		</section>

		<hr class="hairline" />

		<section>
			<h2>Getting in touch</h2>
			<p>
				Nothing is bookable yet. I'm writing the first diagnoses now — if you
				want the sample when it's finished, <a href="/#notify">the waitlist is
				on the homepage</a>. Otherwise:
				<a href="mailto:jon@thejonmartin.com">jon@thejonmartin.com</a>.
			</p>
		</section>

		<hr class="hairline" />

		<footer>
			<p class="links">
				<a href="/writing/">Writing</a>
				<a href="mailto:jon@thejonmartin.com">jon@thejonmartin.com</a>
			</p>
			<p class="copyright">© 2026 Jon Martin.</p>
		</footer>
	</main>
</Base>

<style>
	main { max-width: 680px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2.5rem 1.5rem 4rem; }
	.head { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1.5rem; padding-bottom: 3rem; }
	.kicker { font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--sage); margin: 0; }
	h1 { font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4.5vw, 2.6rem); font-weight: 400; }
	.subhead { font-size: 1.15rem; max-width: 56ch; margin: 0; }
	section { padding: 3rem 0; }
	h2 { font-size: 1.4rem; margin-bottom: 1.25rem; }
	p { margin: 0 0 1.1rem; }
	p:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }
	footer { padding: 3rem 0 0; display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: baseline; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.5rem; }
	.links { display: flex; gap: 1.5rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
	footer a { font-family: "Courier New", Courier, monospace; text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--plum); }
	.copyright { font-size: 0.85rem; opacity: 0.6; margin: 0; }
</style>
